id: pm-rl-32a9
title: "pm rl lineage: the generation chain with each hop's promotion evidence and the invalidation frontier"
description: "The chain is the artifact an operator and an agent both need, and reconstructing it from individual item reads costs more context than the answer is worth. One view walks the generation chain and prints, per hop, the base checkpoint, the collection runs, the proxy and held-out scores with their gap, the approval that authorized the hop, and whether anything upstream has since invalidated it. A generation may have more than one promoted successor - an operator exploring two training configurations from the same parent produces exactly that - so seed to head is not a unique path and the first draft was wrong to assume it was. Given a head the view renders the single ancestry from the seed to that head; invoked without one it enumerates every head and renders each ancestry separately rather than interleaving them, because a merged view would compute reward-gap deltas across generations that never followed one another. The last column is the one that decides what to do next: a chain that is green to generation four and invalidated from generation two onward means the last two promotions rest on a result that no longer holds. pm rl lineage now renders that state per hop with a distinct reason for each condition."
type: Feature
status: closed
priority: 2
